WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.705
Here's a puzzle.

00:00:01.705 --> 00:00:03.638
A program sets x to 1.

00:00:03.638 --> 00:00:07.327
Then it calls a function that sets x to 2.

00:00:07.327 --> 00:00:08.820
Then it prints x.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.890
Does it print 1, or 2?

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.367
To answer that, you need to know which frame a variable lives in.

00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:18.240
That's where this part ends up.

00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:23.033
This is Lecture 4, part 2: parameters and scope.

00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:27.207
We'll pass several arguments, by position and by name,

00:00:27.207 --> 00:00:31.597
give parameters default values, return several values at once,

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:34.783
and then sort out local and global variables.

00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:38.445
A function can have several parameters.

00:00:38.445 --> 00:00:40.807
AddTwo has two of them, a and b.

00:00:41.157 --> 00:00:46.623
When we call AddTwo of 3 and 5, the arguments are matched by position.

00:00:46.623 --> 00:00:52.637
The first argument goes to the first parameter, and the second to the second.

00:00:53.237 --> 00:00:58.416
So the new frame starts with a equal to 3 and b equal to 5.

00:00:58.416 --> 00:01:02.190
The body computes total, 8, and returns it.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.877
Back in the global frame, x is bound to 8.

00:01:06.227 --> 00:01:10.478
The number of arguments must match the number of parameters.

00:01:10.478 --> 00:01:14.587
Call AddTwo with one argument, and Python raises an error.

00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:18.809
A parameter can also have a default value.

00:01:18.809 --> 00:01:25.367
PrintArea has two parameters: width, with default 1, and height, with default 2.

00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:29.910
Call it with no arguments, and both defaults are used.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:30.997
The area is 2.

00:01:31.347 --> 00:01:36.809
Call it with 4 and 2.5, and they're matched by position.

00:01:36.809 --> 00:01:38.467
The area is 10.0.

00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:43.089
A call can also name its arguments, like `height = 5`.

00:01:43.089 --> 00:01:45.305
These are keyword arguments.

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:49.657
They're matched by name, so their order doesn't matter.

00:01:50.257 --> 00:01:53.683
Height 5 and width 3 gives an area of 15.

00:01:53.683 --> 00:01:55.438
And you can mix them.

00:01:55.438 --> 00:01:59.617
Name just the width, and height keeps its default.

00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:02.514
You've used keyword arguments already.

00:02:02.514 --> 00:02:08.613
Print has a parameter called `sep`, short for separator: the string put between the values,

00:02:08.613 --> 00:02:12.367
and one called `end`, the string put after the last one.

00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:16.897
With `sep` set to a dash, print shows `a-b-c`.

00:02:16.897 --> 00:02:24.077
With `end` set to an empty string, the next print carries on, on the same line.

00:02:24.867 --> 00:02:27.999
Pause the video and predict.

00:02:27.999 --> 00:02:31.467
What does PrintArea of 3 print?

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:35.003
Width is 3, matched by position.

00:02:35.003 --> 00:02:39.390
Height isn't given, so it keeps its default, 2.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:40.697
The area is 6.

00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:46.067
A function can also return several values, separated by commas.

00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:51.617
SortPair takes two numbers, and returns them in order, the smaller first.

00:02:51.967 --> 00:03:01.172
The caller receives them with a simultaneous assignment: one name for each value.

00:03:01.172 --> 00:03:04.127
`n1, n2 = SortPair(3, 2)`.

00:03:04.727 --> 00:03:08.407
So `n1` is 2, and `n2` is 3.

00:03:08.757 --> 00:03:10.986
The slide calls this function `sort`.

00:03:10.986 --> 00:03:14.963
We call it `SortPair`, because lists already have a `sort` method,

00:03:14.963 --> 00:03:18.157
and the name should say what the function gives back.

00:03:18.934 --> 00:03:19.708
Now, scope.

00:03:19.708 --> 00:03:24.774
The scope of a variable is the part of the program where it can be used.

00:03:25.124 --> 00:03:29.665
A variable created inside a function is a local variable.

00:03:29.665 --> 00:03:34.764
It lives in the call's frame, and disappears when the call ends.

00:03:35.364 --> 00:03:40.209
A variable created outside all functions is a global variable.

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:45.524
It lives in the global frame, and can be read from inside functions.

00:03:46.124 --> 00:03:49.494
Here, `global_var` is 1, in the global frame.

00:03:49.494 --> 00:03:55.485
The call to `f1` creates a frame, and `local_var`, equal to 2, is created there.

00:03:55.485 --> 00:03:58.481
Inside the call, both names can be read.

00:03:58.481 --> 00:04:00.204
So it prints 1, then 2.

00:04:00.554 --> 00:04:05.076
When the call ends, its frame is gone, and `local_var` with it.

00:04:05.076 --> 00:04:08.594
Printing `local_var` outside gives a `NameError`.

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:11.588
Back to our puzzle.

00:04:11.588 --> 00:04:13.808
`x` is 1, globally.

00:04:13.808 --> 00:04:16.847
Then `f1` assigns `x = 2`.

00:04:17.197 --> 00:04:22.049
Assigning to a name inside a function creates a local variable,

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:25.437
even if a global variable has the same name.

00:04:26.037 --> 00:04:29.355
So now there are two variables called `x`.

00:04:29.355 --> 00:04:32.198
One in the global frame, equal to 1.

00:04:32.198 --> 00:04:35.437
And one in the frame of `f1`, equal to 2.

00:04:35.787 --> 00:04:40.334
Inside the call, `print(x)` finds the local one, and prints 2.

00:04:40.334 --> 00:04:42.900
After the call, that frame is gone.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.907
`print(x)` finds the global one, still 1.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:51.437
If you really want to change a global variable from inside a function,

00:04:51.437 --> 00:04:53.940
declare it with the keyword `global`.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:05:00.845
In `increase`, `global x` tells Python that `x` means the global `x`.

00:05:00.845 --> 00:05:04.645
So `x = x + 1` changes the global frame.

00:05:04.645 --> 00:05:06.450
Both prints show 2.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.921
But use `global` rarely.

00:05:08.921 --> 00:05:14.312
A function that changes globals is hard to test and to reuse,

00:05:14.312 --> 00:05:19.880
because its result depends on state outside it, and changes it.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.976
Instead, pass values in as arguments, and send results out with `return`.

00:05:25.976 --> 00:05:28.309
`increased(x)` returns `x + 1`,

00:05:28.309 --> 00:05:31.320
and the caller decides where to keep it.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.779
So why write functions at all?

00:05:34.779 --> 00:05:35.940
Four reasons.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:41.252
Organise your code into paragraphs, each one complete thought with a name.

00:05:41.252 --> 00:05:44.605
Don't repeat yourself: write it once and reuse it,

00:05:44.605 --> 00:05:47.421
and a bug fixed there is fixed everywhere.

00:05:47.421 --> 00:05:50.170
Break up anything that grows too complex.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:52.450
And build a library you can share.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:59.080
And one pattern to keep: compute in a function, and talk to the user in `main`.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.720
The slides' practice problem: compute the pay for some hours at an hourly rate.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:09.160
Hours over 40 are paid at 1.5 times the rate.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:13.741
`ComputePay` takes hours and rate, and returns the pay.

00:06:13.741 --> 00:06:16.510
`main` does the input and the print.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:22.380
With 45 hours at 10 an hour, the pay is 475.

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:30.700
And because `ComputePay` returns its answer, we can check it directly, without typing anything.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:33.050
`ComputePay(40, 10)` is 400.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:35.650
`ComputePay(45, 10)` gives 475.

00:06:36.434 --> 00:06:37.834
Let's sum up.

00:06:38.184 --> 00:06:42.125
Arguments are matched to parameters by position or by name,

00:06:42.125 --> 00:06:44.864
and a parameter can have a default value.

00:06:45.214 --> 00:06:50.034
A variable assigned inside a function is local to that call's frame.

00:06:50.034 --> 00:06:52.374
Globals live in the global frame.

00:06:52.724 --> 00:06:56.244
Prefer parameters and return values to `global`.

00:06:56.594 --> 00:06:58.215
Here's one to try.

00:06:58.215 --> 00:07:04.520
Trace `AddThree(1, 2, 3)`, when a global called `total` is already 10.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:07.222
What does the last line print?

00:07:07.222 --> 00:07:08.754
Draw both frames.

00:07:09.304 --> 00:07:12.960
In part 3, we look inside strings: indexing, slicing,

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.064
and the methods that every string comes with.

